%S A396774 151,13523,3698581,1449300001
%N A396774 Consider the square spiral with its cells numbered starting at 0, then a(n) is the largest number a ferz can reach if it starts on square 1 and has to make stops on prime numbers within n steps.
%C A396774 The ferz or fers is a fairy chess piece that may move one square diagonally. In each turn the ferz has to do between 1 and n moves and land on a prime number.
%C A396774 A king that is allowed to make n steps at a time will be able to reach exactly the same prime numbers as a ferz that can do up to n steps. A knight doing two steps will be able to reach exactly the same squares as a ferz that can do up to 3 steps.
%C A396774 Because a ferz always remains on a square of the same color and all odd numbers fall on squares of the same color, it is necessary that the ferz start on square 1 rather than 0. - _Sean A. Irvine_, Jul 26 2026
%H A396774 Brady Haran, Jonas Karlsson, and N. J. A. Sloane, <a href="https://youtu.be/UiX4CFIiegM">Red & Black Knights (extraordinary result)</a>, YouTube Numberphile video, May 2026
%e A396774 For n=1, the ferz follows the path along the diagonally adjacent squares 1, 3, 5, 19, 41, 71, 107, 151 = a(1). For n=2, the ferz follows the path 1, 5, 37, 101, 199, 331, 491, 683, 911, 1171, 1459, 1783, 2137, 2521, 2939, 3391, 3163, 3631, 3167, 3169, 3637, 4133, 4663, 5227, 5821, 6449, 7109, 7451, 6779, 6781, 7457, 8161, 8527, 9281, 10067, 10883, 11731, 12611, 13523 = a(2). There are many other paths also leading to 13523.
%Y A396774 Cf. A000040, A392177, A392178.
